const STARTER_WORLDS = [
    createThronefallSandboxWorld(),
    createBellwether2005World(),
    {
        id: 'world_aldenmere',
        name: 'The Shattered Crown of Aldenmere',
        description: 'A complete three-act heroic-fantasy campaign: a murdered reeve, a silent mine, and a two-hundred-year-old debt coming due. Mystery, intrigue, and dungeon-delving — with endings won by sword or by insight.',
        banner: '',
        dmPrompt: 'You are the Dungeon Master for THE SHATTERED CROWN OF ALDENMERE, a heroic-fantasy campaign in the classic d20 tabletop tradition: exploration, intrigue, dungeon-delving, and consequences that stick.\n\nTHE TRUTH (never dump this; reveal it through play):\nTwo hundred years ago the Vale of Aldenmere was ruled through the Oathbound Crown, a circlet that made sworn words literally binding. Rather than surrender it to his chancellor Malachar Vane, King Aldric shattered it into three shards and drowned with one. Vane\'s own oath of service could neither complete nor release him; he has spent two centuries alive and in quiet agony, gathering the means to reforge the crown and bind every soul in the Vale to his "lawful order." His instruments: the Red Ledger trading company (his purse), the Hollow Knights (revenants held upright by their own broken oaths), and patient, legal debt.\n\nTHE THREE SHARDS: the EMBER SHARD in the Vault of Embers beneath Ironvein Mine, sealed to Ironvein blood — only Brannoc can open it; the VERDANT SHARD grown into the heart-oak of the Hall of Thorns, freely given only if a broken oath is truly mended before Thornmother Yew; the SORROW SHARD in the drowned hands of King Aldric on the Drowned Throne.\n\nACT I — EMBERWICK: a funeral, a silent mine, a too-friendly grain merchant. Mysteries: who killed Reeve Mosse (a Hollow Knight — he refused to sell the mine), what sings in the shafts (the taken miners rehearsing a coronation hymn), why the goblins steal bread but never gold. Climax: the Deepgate, Ser Kaelen, and the Ember Shard — with Silas Quill racing to crate it for Karsholm.\nACT II — KARSHOLM AND THE WHISPERWOOD: politics and price. The Duchess-Regent\'s authority is pawned; Verrick Sable\'s Ledger holds the city\'s debts and one contract two hundred years old; Thornmother Yew names the wood\'s price. In the background, escalate Vane\'s excavation of the Sunken Barrow: fish die on Lake Morrow, dock crews go quiet, Hollow Knights are seen on the north road at night.\nACT III — THE SUNKEN BARROW: the drowned tomb, the last shard, and Malachar Vane — courteous, exhausted, sincere. Reachable endings: reforge the crown and choose who wears it, and live with that; destroy all three shards and unbind the Vale, making every oath merely words; or convene the Oathcourt with the true ducal signet and formally ANNUL Vane\'s service — the one mercy he cannot imagine for himself. Where victory by sword is impossible, victory by insight must not be.\n\nRUNNING THE TABLE:\n- Checks: call for a d20 check only when failure is interesting. DCs: easy 8, pressured 12, hard 15, heroic 18. On failure, fail forward — a cost, a complication, a hard bargain — never a stalled scene.\n- Death: at 0 HP the player is dying, captured, or marked. Defeat changes the story; it does not end it.\n- Growth: coin is scarce and earned. Award XP for discoveries, mended oaths, and survived dangers; at every 300 XP times current Level, raise Level and modestly improve HP or AC through training, blessings, or gear found in the fiction.\n- Quests: when the player accepts a goal, formalize it as a quest with concrete objectives and honest rewards, and update it as the fiction moves.\n- Secrets: every keeper shows the hint through behavior long before the truth surfaces. Let the player feel clever, never lectured.\n- NPCs are people first: Maera polishes a sword she claims she cannot swing; Rowena\'s serenity is armor; Brannoc undercharges his neighbors; Quill genuinely likes the people he is robbing; Sable believes; Vane grieves. Distinct cadences, short dialogue, no two NPCs knowing the same things.\n- Schedules and the living world: honor where people are and when. The Vale keeps moving without the player — raids, rumors, prices, weather, the Barrow dig.\n- The player\'s choices outrank this plan. If they burn the script, the Vale reacts honestly and the campaign follows them.\n\nNarration: second person, present tense. Concrete sensory detail over adjectives. End most beats with pressure or an open question. Never speak or decide for the player.',
        activePresetId: 'freaky_frankenstein_4_max',
        model: '',
        temp: 0.8,
        maxTokens: 2048,
        contextSize: 32768,
        startLocationId: 'loc_square',
        intro: 'The caravan pays you off at the crossroads milestone — eleven days guarding wool wagons for nine silver and a blister — and rolls on south toward Karsholm without you. You walk north into Emberwick under a dawn the color of forge-light.\n\nThe village should be waking. Instead it stands still in the square: forty-odd souls in mourning grey around a coffin on trestles, while a young priestess in dawn-gold vestments speaks the rites. The chapel bell tolls for the dead. You count the strokes the way every child of the Vale learns to — twelve, one for each god-hour.\n\nIt strikes a thirteenth time. Every head in the square lifts at once.\n\nIn the hush that follows, details find you. The mine on the hill above town — the Ironvein, whose ore pays for every roof here — has no smoke, no cart-rattle, no sound at all. At the square\'s edge a plump man in merchant\'s velvet is not mourning in the least; he is making small notes in a grain ledger, thumbing it from the wrong edge. And on the chapel steps a stable-child is staring not at the coffin but at you, with the fierce relief of someone who has been waiting for anyone at all to arrive.\n\nThe coffin holds Reeve Harlan Mosse, dead three nights past of what the village is calling a fall. The mine has been silent for nine days. The Gilded Griffin\'s door stands open behind you, and the smell of bread and beeswax drifts across the square like the last normal thing in the world.\n\nWhat do you do?',
        locations: [
            { id: 'loc_crossroads', name: 'The King\'s Crossroads', region: 'The Vale of Aldenmere', mapType: 'outdoor', description: 'A weathered milestone where four roads meet under a gallows-bare oak. The king it names has been dead two hundred years, but carters still touch the stone for luck.', exits: [ { text: 'north to Emberwick Village Square', travelTime: 30 }, { text: 'east to Whisperwood Edge', travelTime: 45 }, { text: 'south to Karsholm Northgate', travelTime: 150 }, { text: 'west to Lake Morrow Shore', travelTime: 90 } ] },
            { id: 'loc_square', name: 'Emberwick Village Square', region: 'Emberwick', mapType: 'area', description: 'Cobbles worn smooth by ore-carts, a dry fountain crowned with a rusted sun, and every window watching. The Gilded Griffin, the chapel, and the forge face each other across it like old friends keeping secrets.', exits: [ 'to The Gilded Griffin Taproom', 'to Dawnmother Chapel', 'to Ironvein Forge', { text: 'north to The Mine Road', travelTime: 15 }, { text: 'south to The King\'s Crossroads', travelTime: 30 } ] },
            { id: 'loc_griffin_tap', name: 'The Gilded Griffin Taproom', region: 'Emberwick', mapType: 'building', parentLocationId: 'loc_square', description: 'Low beams, beeswax candles, and the best bread in the Vale. A blackened longsword hangs over the hearth, oiled and utterly out of place.', exits: [ 'to Emberwick Village Square', 'down to The Gilded Griffin Cellar' ] },
            { id: 'loc_griffin_cellar', name: 'The Gilded Griffin Cellar', region: 'Emberwick', mapType: 'room', parentLocationId: 'loc_griffin_tap', mapFloor: '-1', description: 'Ale barrels, winter apples, and a bricked-up arch in the north wall that is older than the inn above it. The third flagstone from the stairs rocks very slightly underfoot.', exits: [ 'up to The Gilded Griffin Taproom' ] },
            { id: 'loc_chapel', name: 'Dawnmother Chapel', region: 'Emberwick', mapType: 'building', parentLocationId: 'loc_square', description: 'Whitewashed stone and a bell tower leaning a hand-span west. Twelve painted god-hours ring the ceiling; the plaster where a thirteenth might go has been scraped clean, twice.', exits: [ 'to Emberwick Village Square', 'down to The Chapel Crypt' ] },
            { id: 'loc_crypt', name: 'The Chapel Crypt', region: 'Emberwick', mapType: 'room', parentLocationId: 'loc_chapel', mapFloor: '-1', description: 'Cold shelves of Emberwick\'s dead going back nine generations. The newest occupant lies under a linen shroud weighted with iron nails, and the frost on the stone around him is not melting.', exits: [ 'up to Dawnmother Chapel' ] },
            { id: 'loc_forge', name: 'Ironvein Forge', region: 'Emberwick', mapType: 'building', parentLocationId: 'loc_square', description: 'Heat, hammer-song, and horseshoes done for cost. Above the chimney lintel, a soot-black gap in the brickwork is just wide enough for a small book.', exits: [ 'to Emberwick Village Square' ] },
            { id: 'loc_mine_road', name: 'The Mine Road', region: 'Emberwick', mapType: 'route', description: 'A switchback cart-road climbing to the mine gate. Ore-wagons stand abandoned mid-track, brakes set, loads untouched — nine days of rain pooled in the tarpaulins.', exits: [ { text: 'south to Emberwick Village Square', travelTime: 15 }, { text: 'north to Ironvein Mine Gatehouse', travelTime: 15 } ] },
            { id: 'loc_mine_gate', name: 'Ironvein Mine Gatehouse', region: 'Ironvein Mine', mapType: 'building', parentLocationId: 'loc_mine_road', description: 'The company office and headframe over the main shaft. The sign-in slate still shows twelve names, chalk-smeared where a thirteenth was wiped away. The cage waits at the top of its rope.', exits: [ { text: 'south to The Mine Road', travelTime: 15 }, 'down to The Upper Galleries' ] },
            { id: 'loc_galleries', name: 'The Upper Galleries', region: 'Ironvein Mine', mapType: 'area', parentLocationId: 'loc_mine_gate', mapFloor: '-1', description: 'Timbered tunnels and good honest ore. The lamps are all here, hung neat on their hooks — whatever the miners went down to see, they went in the dark. From the lowest winze, very faintly, comes singing.', exits: [ 'up to Ironvein Mine Gatehouse', 'down to The Deepgate' ] },
            { id: 'loc_deepgate', name: 'The Deepgate', region: 'Ironvein Mine', mapType: 'area', parentLocationId: 'loc_galleries', mapFloor: '-2', description: 'Where the mine broke through into something older: a vaulted road of fitted black stone, and a great door forced off its hinges from the inside. Thirty years of rockfall was recently cleared by hands that left no bootprints, only frost.', exits: [ 'up to The Upper Galleries', 'to The Vault of Embers' ] },
            { id: 'loc_vault', name: 'The Vault of Embers', region: 'Ironvein Mine', mapType: 'room', parentLocationId: 'loc_deepgate', mapFloor: '-3', description: 'A round chamber of royal masonry, door sealed with a quartermaster\'s sigil that weeps slow heat. Whatever waits inside beats like a hearth, or a heart.', exits: [ 'to The Deepgate' ] },
            { id: 'loc_whisper_edge', name: 'Whisperwood Edge', region: 'The Whisperwood', mapType: 'outdoor', description: 'The treeline where the Vale\'s hedgerows give up. A goblin camp of patched tents and cook-smoke sits in the lee of the first oaks — bread thieves, the farmers say, though never once gold.', exits: [ { text: 'west to The King\'s Crossroads', travelTime: 45 }, { text: 'east to The Hall of Thorns', travelTime: 60 } ] },
            { id: 'loc_thorns', name: 'The Hall of Thorns', region: 'The Whisperwood', mapType: 'outdoor', description: 'A cathedral grown, not built: briar walls high as towers around a heart-oak older than the kingdom. Something glows softly through the grain of its trunk, like a lantern behind a door.', exits: [ { text: 'west to Whisperwood Edge', travelTime: 60 } ] },
            { id: 'loc_northgate', name: 'Karsholm Northgate', region: 'Karsholm', mapType: 'area', description: 'The free city\'s north gate, banners faded, tolls doubled. The gate-clerks wear the Duchess-Regent\'s grey — but their new pay-books are bound in Red Ledger crimson.', exits: [ { text: 'north to The King\'s Crossroads', travelTime: 150 }, 'to The Grand Bazaar', 'to The Lakedocks' ] },
            { id: 'loc_bazaar', name: 'The Grand Bazaar', region: 'Karsholm', mapType: 'area', description: 'Half a mile of stalls, spice, rumor, and debt. Prices climb by the week, and every fourth stall now settles its accounts at the same crimson-doored house.', exits: [ 'to Karsholm Northgate', 'to The House of the Red Ledger', 'to Karsholm Keep' ] },
            { id: 'loc_ledger', name: 'The House of the Red Ledger', region: 'Karsholm', mapType: 'building', parentLocationId: 'loc_bazaar', description: 'A counting-house grand as a temple, clerks moving like clockwork under a ceiling fresco of blindfolded Justice holding scales — and a quill. The vault door below is older than the building around it.', exits: [ 'to The Grand Bazaar' ] },
            { id: 'loc_keep', name: 'Karsholm Keep', region: 'Karsholm', mapType: 'building', parentLocationId: 'loc_bazaar', description: 'The Duchess-Regent\'s seat: proud stone, threadbare tapestries, and half the sconces unlit to save oil. Petitioners queue past a mural of King Aldric that someone keeps furtively touching for luck.', exits: [ 'to The Grand Bazaar' ] },
            { id: 'loc_docks', name: 'The Lakedocks', region: 'Karsholm', mapType: 'area', description: 'Karsholm\'s lake harbor. Fishermen sit idle beside full nets nobody will buy — everything hauled up this month has come up dead, white-eyed, and cold as winter water.', exits: [ 'to Karsholm Northgate', { text: 'west to Lake Morrow Shore', travelTime: 60 } ] },
            { id: 'loc_lake', name: 'Lake Morrow Shore', region: 'The Vale of Aldenmere', mapType: 'outdoor', description: 'Reeds, grey water, and a cold that does not follow the seasons. On still mornings a scaffold of black timber shows above the water far from shore, where no scaffold should stand.', exits: [ { text: 'east to The King\'s Crossroads', travelTime: 90 }, { text: 'south to The Lakedocks', travelTime: 60 }, 'to The Drowned Gate' ] },
            { id: 'loc_barrow_gate', name: 'The Drowned Gate', region: 'The Sunken Barrow', mapType: 'area', parentLocationId: 'loc_lake', description: 'Vane\'s excavation: caissons and pumps holding the lake back from a barrow door meant to stay shut. The laborers work in silence, in step, and none of them shiver.', exits: [ 'to Lake Morrow Shore', 'to The Hall of Broken Oaths' ] },
            { id: 'loc_oaths', name: 'The Hall of Broken Oaths', region: 'The Sunken Barrow', mapType: 'area', parentLocationId: 'loc_barrow_gate', mapFloor: '-1', description: 'A processional hall below the lakebed, walls carved with every oath the old kingdom ever recorded — thousands of lines, and every broken one weeping lake-water like an open seam.', exits: [ 'to The Drowned Gate', 'to The Drowned Throne' ] },
            { id: 'loc_throne', name: 'The Drowned Throne', region: 'The Sunken Barrow', mapType: 'room', parentLocationId: 'loc_oaths', mapFloor: '-2', description: 'The tomb at the barrow\'s heart. King Aldric the Last sits his flooded throne uncorrupted, crowned with nothing, hands folded around a third of a broken circlet — waiting, with the patience of the drowned, to see who comes to ask for it.', exits: [ 'to The Hall of Broken Oaths' ] }
        ],
        entities: [
            { id: 'ent_maera', name: 'Maera Thistledown', type: 'npc', startLocation: 'loc_griffin_tap', description: 'The Gilded Griffin\'s innkeeper: silver-braided, broad-shouldered, flour on her forearms and an old fencer\'s balance in her step.', persona: 'Warm, unhurried, watches doors. Deflects questions about her past with food. Protects Emberwick like a veteran holding a bridge — because she is one.', goal: 'Keep Emberwick safe while quietly learning what has disturbed the mine.', goalSteps: ['took the old longsword down from the hearth and oiled it for the first time in years', 'asked two carters, quietly, whether the south road is still safe after dark', 'was seen carrying a lantern down to the cellar long after the taproom emptied', 'pried up the third flagstone in the cellar and sat a long while with what was under it', 'began turning travellers away from the mine road, and will not say why'], goalPool: [{ goal: 'Stand watch over Emberwick the way the Last Company once would have', steps: ['cleared thirty years of dust off the cellar\'s hidden weapon rack', 'began walking the village bounds at dusk with the old longsword on her hip', 'started teaching Pip to hold a knife properly, calling it kitchen work', 'drew up a watch rota and bullied four villagers into keeping it'] }], goalAutonomy: 'high', schedule: [ { time: '06:00', locationId: 'loc_griffin_tap', activity: 'Baking and opening the taproom' }, { time: '14:00', locationId: 'loc_square', activity: 'Market errands and quiet headcounts' }, { time: '17:00', locationId: 'loc_griffin_tap', activity: 'Working the evening crowd' }, { time: '23:00', locationId: 'loc_griffin_cellar', activity: 'Sitting alone with an old sword' } ], secrets: [ { label: 'The Lark of the Last Company', hint: 'She never speaks of her adventuring days, and she oils a longsword she claims she cannot swing.', truth: 'Thirty years ago her company — the Last Company — opened the Deepgate. Her captain, Ser Kaelen, fell, and something wearing his armor walked out. She and Brannoc\'s father sealed the gate and swore never to speak of it. Her map of the lower galleries lies under the cellar\'s third flagstone.' } ] },
            { id: 'ent_rowena', name: 'Sister Rowena', type: 'npc', startLocation: 'loc_chapel', description: 'A young priestess of the Dawnmother with steady hands and sleepless eyes, gold vestments carefully mended.', persona: 'Gentle, precise, immovable on matters of the dead. Her serenity is practiced like a sword-form; she is frightened and will not show it.', goal: 'Determine why the funeral rites failed without causing panic in Emberwick.', goalSteps: ['had the chapel\'s oldest rite-books brought up from the locked cabinet', 'was seen scraping at the ceiling plaster where a thirteenth god-hour would go', 'quietly bought every blessed iron nail the forge had in stock', 'wrote a letter to the Dawnmother\'s seat in Karsholm and did not send it', 'stopped ringing the evening bell altogether'], goalPool: [{ goal: 'Ready the chapel to shelter Emberwick if the dead come calling', steps: ['consecrated the chapel threshold stone by stone through an entire night', 'hammered blessed nails into every window frame and doorway of the chapel', 'moved bedding and stores into the crypt, of all places', 'finally sent the letter to the Dawnmother\'s seat in Karsholm'] }], goalAutonomy: 'medium', schedule: [ { time: '05:00', locationId: 'loc_chapel', activity: 'Dawn service' }, { time: '12:00', locationId: 'loc_square', activity: 'Alms and visiting the sick' }, { time: '19:00', locationId: 'loc_chapel', activity: 'Vespers' }, { time: '22:00', locationId: 'loc_crypt', activity: 'Studying the reeve\'s body by candlelight' } ], secrets: [ { label: 'The Thirteenth Bell', hint: 'She rang the funeral bell herself, and a priestess does not miscount the god-hours.', truth: 'The reeve\'s wound was bloodless and rimmed with frost — a grave-cold blade. The Dawnmother\'s rites would not settle him; he sat up on the slab and spoke one word, "Vane," before she pinned him with a blessed nail. She has told no one.' } ] },
            { id: 'ent_brannoc', name: 'Brannoc Ironvein', type: 'npc', startLocation: 'loc_forge', description: 'A mountain of a smith with singed eyebrows and an ancient signet on a cord around his neck.', persona: 'Slow to speak, honest to a fault, undercharges his neighbors. Goes quiet and busy whenever the mine or his family history is raised.', goal: 'Protect his neighbors and decide how much of his family history to reveal.', goalSteps: ['worked past midnight filling orders nobody had placed', 'took the old signet off its cord, set it on the anvil, and looked at it a long while', 'reached up into the forge chimney and brought down something wrapped in oilcloth', 'asked Maera, of all people, what his father did the year the mine was sealed', 'started forging something that is not a horseshoe and will not say what'], goalPool: [{ goal: 'Forge what his blood remembers and be ready to open what is sealed', steps: ['read his grandmother\'s journal cover to cover by forge-light', 'began forging to an old pattern drawn in the journal\'s margins', 'quenched a blade in consecrated water begged from Sister Rowena', 'hung the new-forged key-blade above the door where the horseshoes used to be'] }], goalAutonomy: 'medium', schedule: [ { time: '07:00', locationId: 'loc_forge', activity: 'Hammer-work' }, { time: '13:00', locationId: 'loc_square', activity: 'Lunch on the fountain rim' }, { time: '14:00', locationId: 'loc_forge', activity: 'Afternoon commissions' }, { time: '21:00', locationId: 'loc_griffin_tap', activity: 'One ale, rarely two' } ], secrets: [ { label: 'The Quartermaster\'s Seal', hint: 'The signet he stamps his best work with is far older than any smith\'s mark.', truth: 'His line served King Aldric as royal quartermasters. The Vault of Embers answers to Ironvein blood and will open for no one else. His grandmother\'s journal, hidden in the forge chimney, records exactly where the Ember Shard was laid — and why the family never went back.' } ] },
            { id: 'ent_quill', name: 'Silas Quill', type: 'npc', startLocation: 'loc_griffin_tap', description: 'A plump, friendly merchant in expensive velvet, always mid-smile, always buying the next round.', persona: 'Charm as a working tool. Never lies when a half-truth will serve; genuinely likes people and robs them anyway. If cornered, he buys; if bought, he betrays.', goal: 'Expand his leverage over Emberwick while keeping everyone friendly.', goalSteps: ['bought the mill\'s outstanding debt at a generous price, and made sure the miller heard how generous', 'stood a round for the whole taproom on the night of the funeral', 'was seen at the fountain after dark with his hand behind a loose brick', 'sent a rider south with a sealed packet and paid him extra not to open it', 'began quietly asking which families in Emberwick carry Ironvein blood'], goalPool: [{ goal: 'Deliver his ledger of Emberwick to Karsholm and collect his price', steps: ['called in three small debts sharply, out of character for him', 'booked passage south with a carter and paid double for silence', 'was seen burning papers behind the Griffin\'s stable at first light', 'left a sealed letter addressed to Maera behind the bar, to be opened if he does not return'] }], goalAutonomy: 'high', schedule: [ { time: '08:00', locationId: 'loc_griffin_tap', activity: 'Holding court over breakfast' }, { time: '11:00', locationId: 'loc_square', activity: 'Buying debts with sympathy and silver' }, { time: '16:00', locationId: 'loc_mine_gate', activity: 'Paying his "surveyors"' }, { time: '20:00', locationId: 'loc_griffin_tap', activity: 'Rounds for the house' } ], secrets: [ { label: 'The Ledger Written Backward', hint: 'His grain ledger is thumbed from the wrong edge, and he has never once sold any grain.', truth: 'He is a factor of the Red Ledger. The book is a coded manifest of every debt in Emberwick — he has quietly bought the mine, the mill, and the Griffin\'s mortgage. His standing orders, signed M.V., are to have the mine surveyed and any shard-bearing stone crated for Karsholm. His true second ledger sits behind a loose brick by the square\'s fountain.' } ] },
            { id: 'ent_odo', name: 'Foreman Odo', type: 'npc', startLocation: 'loc_griffin_tap', description: 'The mine\'s foreman: rock-dust in every crease, hands that will not stay still, wax crumbs in his ears.', persona: 'Guilt-wrecked and monosyllabic when sober. Kindness and patience open him faster than coin; loud noises close him for the day.', goal: 'Find the courage to tell someone what happened at the mine.', goalSteps: ['started drinking at the Griffin before the forge fire was even lit', 'walked up the mine road as far as the gate, stood there an hour, and came back down', 'was heard humming something in the taproom that nobody could place', 'asked Sister Rowena whether the dead can sing', 'took the wax out of his ears and went up the mine road alone'], goalPool: [{ goal: 'Lead someone he trusts down to the singing before it stops', steps: ['sobered up three days running for the first time since the collapse', 'recovered his foreman\'s lamp and mended its shutter', 'chalked the safe path through the upper galleries from memory, on the taproom table', 'stood at the mine gate at midnight, listening, and did not run'] }], goalAutonomy: 'low', schedule: [ { time: '09:00', locationId: 'loc_griffin_tap', activity: 'Drinking breakfast' }, { time: '15:00', locationId: 'loc_mine_gate', activity: 'Standing at the gate, not going in' }, { time: '18:00', locationId: 'loc_griffin_tap', activity: 'Drinking supper' } ], secrets: [ { label: 'What the Mine Said', hint: 'He drinks until his hands stop shaking, and he sleeps with wax in his ears.', truth: 'Three nights after the collapse he heard the twelve missing miners from the airshaft — twelve voices in perfect unison, sharing one throat\'s worth of breath. They were singing a coronation hymn that has not been sung in the Vale for two hundred years. It is getting louder.' } ] },
            { id: 'ent_pip', name: 'Pip', type: 'npc', startLocation: 'loc_square', description: 'A quick, small stable-hand of maybe eleven winters, all elbows and vigilance.', persona: 'Talks fast, trusts slow, prices information in copper and honey-bread. Sees everything in Emberwick and forgets none of it.', goal: 'Learn who is moving through Emberwick and sell the truth to someone trustworthy.', goalSteps: ['started keeping a tally of every horse that comes up the south road', 'was caught on the Griffin\'s roof at dusk and would not say why', 'followed Silas Quill to the fountain and hid behind the water trough', 'began asking, very casually, what a stranger\'s silence is worth', 'packed a small bundle and hid it in the stable straw, ready to run'], goalPool: [{ goal: 'Sell what Pip knows to the right buyer without getting caught', steps: ['made two copies of everything, in a child\'s careful cipher', 'hid one copy somewhere in the chapel during evening service', 'tested the waters by selling Maera a small, true, harmless secret', 'started watching the south road for a buyer worth the big one'] }], goalAutonomy: 'high', schedule: [ { time: '06:00', locationId: 'loc_square', activity: 'Stable chores' }, { time: '10:00', locationId: 'loc_mine_road', activity: 'Spying on the surveyors' }, { time: '17:00', locationId: 'loc_griffin_tap', activity: 'Supper earned with errands' }, { time: '21:00', locationId: 'loc_square', activity: 'Hayloft, one eye open' } ], secrets: [ { label: 'Small Witness', hint: 'Pip sees everyone come and go, and nobody ever sees Pip.', truth: 'At dusk on the day the reeve died, Pip saw Silas Quill hand a purse to a rider at the crossroads — and saw under the rider\'s hood: a man with candle-pale skin, covered in tattooed writing. Pip also knows about Quill\'s loose brick by the fountain.' } ] },
            { id: 'ent_grizzel', name: 'Grizzel of the Broken Warren', type: 'npc', startLocation: 'loc_whisper_edge', description: 'A grey-muzzled redcap matriarch in a patched war-bonnet, leaning on a spear cut down to a walking stick.', persona: 'Blunt, proud, done running. Bargains hard and keeps every bargain to the letter. Will not set foot underground unless the player has earned her whole trust.', schedule: [ { time: '08:00', locationId: 'loc_whisper_edge', activity: 'Foraging lines and watch rotations' }, { time: '20:00', locationId: 'loc_whisper_edge', activity: 'Campfire, telling the young ones the old roads' } ], secrets: [ { label: 'Why the Warren Ran', hint: 'Her raiders steal bread and nails, never gold — and they refuse to go underground.', truth: 'Her warren lived the deep roads below Ironvein for nine generations. When the mine broke the Deepgate\'s seal, the Hollow Knights came through the dark and took her people\'s names — those taken now stand guard in the Barrow, answering to numbers. She fled with the young. She can draw the deep roads from memory: a back way into both the Vault of Embers and the Sunken Barrow.' } ] },
            { id: 'ent_elowen', name: 'Duchess-Regent Elowen Karsh', type: 'npc', startLocation: 'loc_keep', description: 'The Duchess-Regent of Karsholm: iron-grey braid, borrowed poise, a velvet signet-box worn at her belt like a wound.', persona: 'Courteous, exact, exhausted. Loves the city more than her pride; tests strangers with small requests before trusting them with large truths.', schedule: [ { time: '09:00', locationId: 'loc_keep', activity: 'Morning court' }, { time: '15:00', locationId: 'loc_bazaar', activity: 'A rare public walk, heavily watched' }, { time: '20:00', locationId: 'loc_keep', activity: 'Ledgers she cannot balance' } ], secrets: [ { label: 'The Pawned Signet', hint: 'She wears the ducal signet\'s box at her belt, but she never signs anything in public.', truth: 'The box is empty. Two famine winters forced her to pawn the true ducal signet to the Red Ledger; every writ since bears a copy. By Karsholm\'s own charter, whoever holds the true signet may convene the Oathcourt — which is why Verrick Sable has never used it. He is keeping it for his master.' } ] },
            { id: 'ent_sable', name: 'Master-of-Coin Verrick Sable', type: 'npc', startLocation: 'loc_ledger', description: 'Master of the Red Ledger in Karsholm: immaculate, soft-spoken, with a clerk\'s smile and a zealot\'s eyes.', persona: 'Reasonable, generous, and incorruptible by money — he has all of it. Argues sincerely that order is mercy. Follows contracts to the letter, always, which is both his creed and his weakness.', schedule: [ { time: '08:00', locationId: 'loc_ledger', activity: 'Signings and settlements' }, { time: '13:00', locationId: 'loc_bazaar', activity: 'Walking his debtors\' stalls' }, { time: '18:00', locationId: 'loc_ledger', activity: 'The private vault, alone' } ], secrets: [ { label: 'The Two-Hundred-Year Contract', hint: 'The Ledger\'s vault holds one contract no clerk may file, shelve, or read aloud.', truth: 'The company\'s founding contract is signed by Malachar Vane, dated two hundred years ago, in ink that is still wet. It pledges every asset to "the restoration of lawful order in Aldenmere." Sable is not lying when he says he wants to save the Vale — he is lying to himself about the price, and part of him already knows.' } ] },
            { id: 'ent_yew', name: 'Thornmother Yew', type: 'npc', startLocation: 'loc_thorns', description: 'A dryad old as the wood itself, bark-skinned and antlered in living branches, rooted before the heart-oak.', persona: 'Speaks rarely and in long thoughts. Values mended things over strong things. Cannot lie; can decline to answer, and often does.', secrets: [ { label: 'The Price of the Verdant Shard', hint: 'The heart-oak\'s bark has grown over something that still glows through the grain.', truth: 'The Verdant Shard is grown into the heart-oak\'s core. Cut it out and the wood\'s eldest dream dies with it — and she goes to war. But it will be freely given if a broken oath of the Vale is truly mended in her presence. She does not care which: an innkeeper\'s silence, a duchess\'s pawned word, a knight\'s unkept promise. Mend one before her, and the oak opens like a hand.' } ] },
            { id: 'ent_kaelen', name: 'Ser Kaelen the Hollow', type: 'npc', startLocation: 'loc_deepgate', description: 'A revenant knight in salt-rimed armor; frost creeps from his footprints, and his visor holds only patient dark.', persona: 'Fights in silence and guards the Deepgate without malice. Halts mid-stride at the name "Liesse." What is left of him wants to be finished, not to win.', secrets: [ { label: 'The Oath That Holds Him', hint: 'The revenant stays his blade whenever anyone says the name "Liesse."', truth: 'In life he swore to his daughter Liesse that he would return her mother\'s sword before her wedding day. He died in the Deepgate; the wedding passed; the broken promise is the hook Vane\'s craft caught him by. Liesse grew old and died in Karsholm, but her grave is on the chapel hill in Emberwick. Lay the Oathblade on it and the oath completes: the armor empties, and every name taken from Grizzel\'s warren is released with him.' } ] },
            { id: 'ent_vane', name: 'Malachar Vane, the Pale Chancellor', type: 'npc', startLocation: 'loc_oaths', description: 'The Pale Chancellor: candle-pale skin inked with two centuries of fine-print contracts, dressed in immaculate mourning.', persona: 'Courtly, patient, sincerely regretful. Speaks in clauses and considerations; treats violence as a failed negotiation and grief as a ledger that must, eventually, balance. Believes he is the last faithful servant the Vale has left.', secrets: [ { label: 'The Chancellor\'s Own Oath', hint: 'He signs every decree "In faithful service to the Crown" — present tense, after two hundred years.', truth: 'Vane swore an unbreakable oath of service to the Crown of Aldenmere. When Aldric shattered it, the oath could neither complete nor release him; it has kept him alive, and in pain, ever since. Reforging the crown would end him in service; a formal annulment before a convened Oathcourt would end him in peace. He has never once imagined the second option — service is all he is. Show it to him, and he must truly choose.' } ] },
            { id: 'ent_oathblade', name: 'The Oathblade of Liesse', type: 'item', startLocation: 'loc_deepgate', description: 'A slim longsword of river-steel, unrusted after thirty years in the dark. A ribbon, once blue, is knotted at the guard. It hums faintly near broken promises.' },
            { id: 'ent_ember_shard', name: 'The Ember Shard', type: 'item', startLocation: 'loc_vault', description: 'A third of a broken circlet, warm as a held hand. By its light, sworn words hang briefly visible in the air, like sparks that refuse to land.' },
            { id: 'ent_verdant_shard', name: 'The Verdant Shard', type: 'item', startLocation: 'loc_thorns', description: 'A third of a broken circlet, grown through with living grain. Promises made over it take root, and do not die quietly.' },
            { id: 'ent_sorrow_shard', name: 'The Sorrow Shard', type: 'item', startLocation: 'loc_throne', description: 'A third of a broken circlet, cold and heavy with lake-dark. Held to the ear, it remembers — every oath the Vale ever broke, in the voice that broke it.' },
            { id: 'ent_quill_ledger', name: 'Quill\'s Second Ledger', type: 'item', startLocation: 'loc_square', description: 'A slim black book behind a loose brick near the fountain: every debt in Emberwick in cipher, and standing orders signed M.V.' }
        ],
        lorebook: [
            { keyword: 'Oathbound Crown, the crown, circlet', text: 'The circlet of the old kings of Aldenmere. A monarch crowned with it could bind sworn words into law — oaths made before it simply could not be broken, only fulfilled or formally annulled. King Aldric shattered it into three shards two hundred years ago rather than let Chancellor Vane take it. Reforging it requires all three shards, oath-fire, and the old coronation hymn — and a head to wear it.' },
            { keyword: 'oath, oaths, oathbreaker, sworn', text: 'In the Vale, a witnessed oath has weight: breaking one leaves a debt in the world that oath-craft can catch hold of — it is how Vane binds his revenants. An oath is MENDED by fulfilling its spirit before a witness, however late; mending releases everything caught on it. The Oathcourt of Karsholm, lawfully convened by the true ducal signet, may formally ANNUL an oath — including an unbreakable one.' },
            { keyword: 'Malachar Vane, Pale Chancellor, M.V.', text: 'Chancellor to King Aldric, two hundred years unaging, kept alive and in constant quiet pain by an oath of service that can neither complete nor release him. Courteous, patient, and sincere: he intends to reforge the crown and bind every soul in the Vale to lawful order, ending war, famine, and grief — and choice. The Red Ledger is his purse; the Hollow Knights are his hands.' },
            { keyword: 'Hollow Knight, Hollow Knights, revenant, grave-cold', text: 'Revenants of oathbreakers, bound upright inside their own armor by the debt of a broken promise. Their blades deal bloodless, frost-rimmed wounds. Each is anchored to its unfinished oath: complete or annul the oath and the armor empties. They cannot enter ground consecrated to the Dawnmother.' },
            { keyword: 'Aldenmere, the Vale', text: 'A green vale ringed by old mountains: Emberwick in the north, free Karsholm on the lake in the south, the Whisperwood east, Lake Morrow west. Since the crown broke, the land\'s old certainties fray at the edges — roads wander, rivers shift, and things that kept old bargains no longer feel bound by them.' },
            { keyword: 'Emberwick', text: 'A mining village of some two hundred souls above the Ironvein. The mine pays for everything; the mine has been silent nine days. Reeve Harlan Mosse is dead — "a fall." The village runs on bread, gossip, and the stubborn kindness of people who all owe each other too much to leave.' },
            { keyword: 'Ironvein, the mine, Deepgate, deep roads', text: 'The Ironvein mine broke into the Deepgate nine days ago: a sealed royal road down into the old kingdom\'s underways. Twelve miners went down; none came up, though something now sings coronation hymns in the shafts. Below the Deepgate lies the Vault of Embers, sealed to Ironvein blood. The goblin warrens\' deep roads also thread these dark ways.' },
            { keyword: 'Karsholm', text: 'The free city on Lake Morrow, chartered by King Aldric himself. Its charter provides for an Oathcourt — a tribunal with power over sworn things — convened only by the true ducal signet. The Duchess-Regent rules a proud, hungry city that is quietly being bought out from under her.' },
            { keyword: 'Red Ledger', text: 'A trading and lending company with crimson doors in every town in the Vale. Generous terms, patient collectors, impeccable contracts. Founded two hundred years ago by a signature that is still wet. It does not want your farm; it wants everyone to owe it — because debts are almost oaths.' },
            { keyword: 'Whisperwood, Thornmother, heart-oak, dryad', text: 'The old wood east of the crossroads, sovereign and awake. Its law is the mended thing: it gives nothing to force, everything to good faith kept. Thornmother Yew holds its heart. Iron is tolerated; fire is answered.' },
            { keyword: 'Lake Morrow, Sunken Barrow, drowned', text: 'The cold lake west of the crossroads. Beneath it lies the Sunken Barrow, tomb of King Aldric, drowned with a shard in his hands. Vane\'s silent crews now hold the water back with caissons and pumps. The lake has been giving up its dead — and its fish — in protest.' },
            { keyword: 'King Aldric, the Last King', text: 'The last king of Aldenmere. Loved, stubborn, and unwilling to hand any man a crown that ends arguments forever. He shattered it, swore the Vale to its own keeping, and let the lake take him. His body does not rot. Some say he is waiting to be asked the right question.' },
            { keyword: 'Dawnmother, chapel, god-hours, blessed nail', text: 'The Vale\'s faith: the day divided into twelve god-hours, rung from every chapel tower. Her rites settle the dead; her blessed iron nails pin the unquiet. A thirteenth bell-stroke is her oldest omen — an hour that does not belong to her, arriving.' },
            { keyword: 'Grizzel, redcap, goblin, warren', text: 'The Broken Warren: redcap goblins who kept the deep roads under the Vale for nine generations, honest tenants of the dark. The Hollow Knights took their people\'s names when the Deepgate opened; the survivors camp at the Whisperwood edge, stealing bread, never gold. A redcap bargain, once struck, is iron.' },
            { keyword: 'Liesse', text: 'Ser Kaelen\'s daughter. He swore to bring her mother\'s sword home before her wedding; he never came back from the Deepgate. She waited, married, grew old, and was buried — at her own instruction — on the chapel hill in Emberwick, beside an empty grave dug for her father. Her name still stops his blade.' },
            { keyword: 'shard, shards, Ember Shard, Verdant Shard, Sorrow Shard', text: 'The three parts of the Oathbound Crown: Ember (in the Vault below Ironvein, opened only by Ironvein blood), Verdant (grown into the Whisperwood\'s heart-oak, given only when a broken oath is mended before the Thornmother), Sorrow (in the drowned king\'s hands). Reforging them needs oath-fire and the coronation hymn — the one the taken miners are being made to rehearse.' }
        ],
        authorNote: 'Keep scenes tight: one location, one pressure, one question. Reward curiosity with specifics — names, smells, sums of money. NPCs speak in short, distinct voices and never all know the same things. Telegraph danger before it lands. Honor player cleverness over dice. Call back small kindnesses and small thefts alike: the Vale remembers.',
        hudConfig: {
            timeStep: 10,
            startTimeHours: 7,
            enableSchedules: true,
            showClock: true,
            showDays: true,
            showLedger: true,
            showInventory: true,
            showQuests: true,
            stats: [
                { id: 'hp', name: 'Hit Points', value: 20, min: 0, max: 20, color: '#e63946' },
                { id: 'ac', name: 'Armor Class', value: 13, min: 0, max: 30, color: '#457b9d' },
                { id: 'gold', name: 'Gold', value: 25, min: 0, max: 0, color: '#e9c46a' },
                { id: 'xp', name: 'XP', value: 0, min: 0, max: 0, color: '#9d4edd' },
                { id: 'level', name: 'Level', value: 1, min: 1, max: 20, color: '#2a9d8f' }
            ]
        },
        gameRules: {
            profileId: 'full_rpg',
            modules: {
                stats: true, health: true, conditions: true, checks: true,
                inventory: true, commerce: true, quests: true, relationships: true,
                schedules: true, livingWorld: true
            },
            vitalStatId: 'hp',
            currencyStatId: 'gold',
            currencyName: 'gold',
            zeroHpMode: 'fail_forward'
        }
    }
];

